WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:01.478 --> 00:00:02.747
"1980."

00:00:06.440 --> 00:00:11.271
The 80s were all about 
big hair, neon, MTV.

00:00:11.271 --> 00:00:13.923
And then there was this:

00:00:13.923 --> 00:00:17.776
"Every October a hole appears in the 
ozone layer over the south pole."

00:00:17.776 --> 00:00:22.442
"...hole in the ozone shield is the size
of the continental United States."

00:00:22.442 --> 00:00:25.920
"The protective ozone layer is 
being threatened as never before."

00:00:25.920 --> 00:00:28.883
"We are all at risk."

00:00:28.883 --> 00:00:35.085
Scientists warned that humanity was on track
to completely destroy the ozone layer by 2050.

00:00:36.355 --> 00:00:39.254
Without it ecosystems would collapse,

00:00:39.254 --> 00:00:41.700
skin cancer rates would skyrocket,

00:00:41.700 --> 00:00:47.410
and life as we knew it would cease to exist.

00:00:47.410 --> 00:00:52.296
But today, the ozone layer is healing.

00:00:53.857 --> 00:01:00.377
In an unprecedented act, the world came together
to prevent an environmental catastrophe.

00:01:00.377 --> 00:01:02.974
So how did we do it?

00:01:02.974 --> 00:01:12.880
And what can we learn from it?

00:01:12.880 --> 00:01:18.226
The ozone layer is a sort of “belt” around
the Earth made up of gaseous molecules.

00:01:18.226 --> 00:01:19.970
It protects every living thing

00:01:19.970 --> 00:01:24.270
by absorbing two types of 
ultraviolet radiation from the Sun.

00:01:24.802 --> 00:01:29.516
It’s a powerful shield, but 
it’s also fragile.

00:01:29.516 --> 00:01:34.198
In 1985, scientists discovered a 
massive loss of ozone here: 

00:01:34.198 --> 00:01:35.853
right over Antarctica.

00:01:36.565 --> 00:01:41.196
40% of the layer had dissipated,
creating a “hole”.

00:01:41.196 --> 00:01:46.590
Scientists realized the hole formed in the
spring and every year it got worse.

00:01:46.590 --> 00:01:48.428
This was a wake up call.

00:01:48.428 --> 00:01:50.192
It wasn't small and far in the future.

00:01:50.192 --> 00:01:55.335
It was now and way bigger 
than anybody ever imagined.

00:01:55.335 --> 00:01:59.839
That’s Dr. Solomon, an atmospheric chemist.

00:01:59.839 --> 00:02:04.567
In 1986, she flew to Antarctica, 
along with other scientists

00:02:04.567 --> 00:02:07.246
to investigate the cause of the ozone hole.

00:02:07.246 --> 00:02:08.974
“Leading the team is Susan Solomon,

00:02:08.974 --> 00:02:11.500
a young atmospheric chemist 
from Boulder, Colorado."

00:02:11.500 --> 00:02:13.650
You know once you step off the plane in Antarctica,

00:02:13.650 --> 00:02:14.790
if you've never been there before,

00:02:14.790 --> 00:02:17.529
your main goal is to get out 
without getting frostbite.

00:02:18.232 --> 00:02:19.069
"Do you want to do the next one?"

00:02:19.069 --> 00:02:23.710
But what really our goal was to take measurements,

00:02:23.710 --> 00:02:32.258
not just of ozone, but also of different chemicals
that would help to show why it was going away.

00:02:32.258 --> 00:02:37.160
Some scientists released balloons in the sky
to take ozone measurements.

00:02:37.160 --> 00:02:40.000
While others took measurements on the ground.

00:02:40.000 --> 00:02:45.237
And they all came to the same conclusion.

00:02:45.237 --> 00:02:53.250
The biggest problem was chlorine from a man-made
compound called Chlorofluorocarbons, or CFCs.

00:02:53.250 --> 00:02:56.160
On the ground CFCs aren’t harmful.

00:02:56.160 --> 00:03:00.920
But once they float up to the stratosphere
the Sun breaks them down into chlorine.

00:03:00.920 --> 00:03:06.278
They bind with ozone to make 
oxygen and chlorine monoxide.

00:03:06.278 --> 00:03:10.639
Then the loose oxygen atoms 
bump the chlorine atom out,

00:03:10.639 --> 00:03:13.461
freeing it to destroy more ozone molecules.

00:03:13.461 --> 00:03:16.060
And that causes a chain reaction.

00:03:16.060 --> 00:03:20.276
The long lifetime of the chlorofluorocarbons
is a big part of the problem.

00:03:20.276 --> 00:03:25.703
They live anywhere between 50 and 150 years
in our atmosphere so...

00:03:25.703 --> 00:03:28.830
It means that every year that you use

00:03:28.830 --> 00:03:32.590
what you use the year before 
is almost entirely still there.

00:03:32.590 --> 00:03:36.236
So it just builds up and builds up exponentially.

00:03:36.236 --> 00:03:40.660
And back then we used a lot of CFCs.

00:03:40.660 --> 00:03:44.901
The US had already moved away 
from CFCs in aerosol cans.

00:03:44.901 --> 00:03:47.571
But most of the world hadn’t yet.

00:03:47.571 --> 00:03:53.680
And they were still in everything from refrigerators
and air conditioners and styrofoam.

00:03:53.680 --> 00:03:58.460
The entire world needed to make a big change
quickly or we’d face--

00:03:58.460 --> 00:03:59.260
Catastrophe.

00:03:59.260 --> 00:04:00.430
Catastrophe.

00:04:00.430 --> 00:04:02.933
Unmitigated catastrophe.

00:04:02.933 --> 00:04:08.164
In 1987, ozone levels had dropped 
by 50 percent.

00:04:08.164 --> 00:04:14.370
This growing threat led to some of the fastest
collective action on climate we've ever seen.

00:04:14.370 --> 00:04:20.150
So I like to think of it as, there's three
P's that when, they’re met we do very well

00:04:20.150 --> 00:04:22.629
at addressing environmental problems.

00:04:22.629 --> 00:04:23.990
So it was personal.

00:04:23.990 --> 00:04:27.293
It was perceptible and the solutions 
were practical.

00:04:27.293 --> 00:04:32.376
If you've been sunburned, you know that 
skin cancer is not a good thing.

00:04:32.376 --> 00:04:35.000
So everybody understands skin cancer.

00:04:35.000 --> 00:04:37.567
The personal nature of the threat is huge.

00:04:37.567 --> 00:04:41.556
The perceptible was easy to do 
with satellite measurements.

00:04:41.556 --> 00:04:44.756
You can just watch it get 
completely destroyed and go to zero

00:04:44.756 --> 00:04:47.133
where there should have been a lot of ozone.

00:04:47.133 --> 00:04:48.866
And we have practical solutions.

00:04:48.866 --> 00:04:53.146
It was easy to find substitutes for 
chlorofluorocarbons in spray cans that took,

00:04:53.146 --> 00:04:56.500
you know, less than a year to do.

00:04:56.500 --> 00:04:59.675
It was a very straightforward switch,

00:04:59.675 --> 00:05:05.666
And I think the main unifying factor 
in all of that is the public.

00:05:05.666 --> 00:05:10.636
Scientists like Dr. Solomon held 
press conferences to inform the public.

00:05:10.636 --> 00:05:16.759
"I think we will eventually see large scale
depletions of the ozone layer in other latitudes."

00:05:16.759 --> 00:05:20.433
The ozone hole started showing up 
in TV shows and movies.

00:05:20.433 --> 00:05:24.118
“It’s those damn fluorocarbons, they’ve
been kicking the hell out of the ozone.”

00:05:24.118 --> 00:05:26.749
“Macaroni, it will burn off.”

00:05:26.749 --> 00:05:29.630
“Well so will the ozone, eventually.”

00:05:29.630 --> 00:05:34.422
And all this public awareness put pressure
on leaders around the world to act.

00:05:34.422 --> 00:05:39.819
“We are here today because we recognize
that urgent action is necessary.”

00:05:39.819 --> 00:05:43.355
And the Montreal Protocol made it official.

00:05:43.355 --> 00:05:48.009
It recognized “that world-wide emissions
can significantly deplete the ozone layer

00:05:48.009 --> 00:05:52.599
and result in adverse effects on human health
and the environment."

00:05:52.599 --> 00:05:57.597
It listed control measures to reduce ozone
depleting substances in a series of steps.

00:05:57.597 --> 00:06:00.000
Including help for developing countries

00:06:00.000 --> 00:06:03.990
who need alternative technology 
and substituting products.

00:06:05.556 --> 00:06:09.489
Every single country eventually 
signed the protocol.

00:06:09.489 --> 00:06:13.776
Making it the only universal treaty 
to ever be ratified.

00:06:13.776 --> 00:06:18.605
And the most successful environmental agreement
in human history.

00:06:20.970 --> 00:06:25.576
Soon after, the world’s largest CFC producer
began to phase them out.

00:06:26.159 --> 00:06:29.750
Since the protocol went into effect 
on January 1st, 1989

00:06:29.750 --> 00:06:32.386
the consumption of ozone-depleting substances

00:06:32.386 --> 00:06:35.142
 including CFCs, plummeted.

00:06:35.676 --> 00:06:39.949
Today, more than thirty years after 
the Montreal Protocol was signed

00:06:39.949 --> 00:06:45.282
the ozone hole has stopped growing 
and is now shrinking.

00:06:45.282 --> 00:06:49.990
And by 2065 it is expected to 
have recovered completely.

00:06:51.739 --> 00:06:54.737
But there’s more to be done.

00:06:54.737 --> 00:07:00.629
After the CFC ban we began using 
Hydrofluorocarbons or HFCs.

00:07:00.629 --> 00:07:05.026
HFCs don’t deplete the ozone layer but they
are a potent greenhouse gas

00:07:05.026 --> 00:07:07.356
that contributes to climate change.

00:07:07.356 --> 00:07:10.567
And it’s the fastest growing one.

00:07:10.567 --> 00:07:15.279
So in 2016, the Montreal Protocol 
was amended to include HFCs

00:07:15.279 --> 00:07:18.566
and now they are being phased out too.

00:07:18.566 --> 00:07:22.250
But they are only one part 
of a larger issue.

00:07:23.199 --> 00:07:26.892
"Climate change is already happening, 
right here, right now."

00:07:26.892 --> 00:07:31.470
"Experts say that we have until 2030 
to avoid catastrophe."

00:07:31.470 --> 00:07:33.375
"People are suffering."

00:07:33.375 --> 00:07:35.226
"People are dying."

00:07:35.226 --> 00:07:39.476
"Entire ecosystems are collapsing."

00:07:39.476 --> 00:07:43.897
"Unprecedented and even irreversible changes
are happening to this planet."

00:07:43.897 --> 00:07:49.223
"It is beyond any doubt that 
human activity is to blame."

00:07:49.223 --> 00:07:56.229
Climate change, our most challenging environmental
problem is still in need of big solutions.

00:07:56.229 --> 00:08:00.000
So I think people in most parts 
of the world, now understand

00:08:00.000 --> 00:08:02.700
and are concerned about, 
the personal impact.

00:08:02.700 --> 00:08:06.453
They found it to be perceptible.

00:08:06.453 --> 00:08:10.216
And we are finding practical solutions.

00:08:10.216 --> 00:08:13.010
It's not true that we can't do it anymore.

00:08:14.026 --> 00:08:16.387
We need to keep our eye on the ball 
on climate change.

00:08:16.387 --> 00:08:19.449
And if we do we will get the environment 
that we demand.

